{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9Nd9RBfQbigxdQCcWrayW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\timport type {\n2\t  MosaicColor,\n3\t  MosaicDocument,\n4\t  MosaicEngineContext,\n5\t  MosaicSource,\n6\t} from \"@m0saic/types\";\n7\timport { asTemplateId } from \"@m0saic/types\";\n8\timport { toM0String } from \"@m0saic/dsl-stdlib\";\n9\timport {\n10\t  bindProp,\n11\t  bindPropPath,\n12\t  defineMosaicTemplate,\n13\t  definePropsSchema,\n14\t  makeColorTile,\n15\t  placeInsetPieces,\n16\t  tag,\n17\t} from \"@m0saic/template-utils\";\n18\timport type { LayoutConstraint } from \"@m0saic/template-utils\";\n19\timport { textFitsMeasured, withLayoutIntent } from \"../../../_shared/layout\";\n20\timport { budget, ellipsize, textCell, widthOf, wrapFit } from \"../../../_shared/text\";\n21\timport { whyTutorial } from \"../../../_shared/why\";\n22\timport type { WhySpec } from \"../../../_shared/why\";\n23\t\n24\t/**\n25\t * `@one-a-day/dev/bench-delta/v1` — the before/after benchmark card, and\n26\t * whether the change is real.\n27\t *\n28\t * ONE CONCEPT: **the rectangles carry the claim.** Every row's BASELINE bar\n29\t * ends at the same x, so the whole card has ONE vertical rule, and the\n30\t * CANDIDATE bar is drawn relative to its own baseline — so a bar's length is\n31\t * the speedup and nothing else. Ratios, not magnitudes, are the shared\n32\t * channel, which is what lets ns, MB and percent sit honestly on one card;\n33\t * the magnitudes stay as printed text in the label column, where mixed units\n34\t * cost nothing. Under the default smaller-is-better, a bar that crosses the\n35\t * rule is a regression, readable as a shape before any label is read (with\n36\t * `smallerIsBetter: false` the geometry is byte-identical and crossing is the\n37\t * win — the footer states which direction the card is scoring).\n38\t *\n39\t * The second rectangle on each bar is the run-to-run interval — `range`, the\n40\t * same optional field `github-action-benchmark` already carries — and the\n41\t * VERDICT is derived from it, never from the ratio alone: disjoint intervals\n42\t * are `better` / `worse`, overlapping intervals are `within noise`, and a row\n43\t * where EITHER side failed to declare a range gets no verdict at all. That\n44\t * last rule is the opinion this template exists to hold. A candidate run once\n45\t * has not been measured, and silence on one side is not a claim of exactness,\n46\t * so it earns no pill. An explicit `0` IS a claim — a deterministic metric\n47\t * gets its verdict. For contrast, the action's own regression check fires when\n48\t * a number is \"worse than the previous exceeding 200% threshold\", which has no\n49\t * notion of noise at all.\n50\t *\n51\t * What the card computes is weaker than significance, so the footer says so\n52\t * out loud: *a verdict means the two +/- ranges do not overlap*. A card that\n53\t * names its own test can be argued with; one that only prints a verdict\n54\t * cannot.\n55\t *\n56\t * The rule that bites: **svg text never wraps or shrinks by itself, and\n57\t * benchmark names are arbitrary user strings** (\"My Custom Smaller Is Better\n58\t * Benchmark - CPU Load\" is the real example in the action's own README). Every\n59\t * cell is measured against the bundled font, one shared size per COLUMN so\n60\t * neighbouring rows never differ, and the degrade ladder is: shrink, wrap to\n61\t * two lines, WIDEN the label column, drop whole lines, and only then — at the\n62\t * 480x270 contract floor, where the box holds one line of 7px type and the\n63\t * alternative is dropping the benchmark — ellipsize. The footer degrades by\n64\t * dropping whole clauses, lowest value first, so its disclosures survive; the\n65\t * values line drops the unit before it would ever truncate a digit.\n66\t *\n67\t * Nothing here is drawn that carries no data, and that is a performance\n68\t * contract as much as a design one: a decorative full-track wash under every\n69\t * bar kept the tiles off the engine's grid sheet and pushed the overlay chain\n70\t * to 27 at five rows — past the ~25 where ffmpeg SILENTLY degrades inline\n71\t * masks, which is every glyph on this card. Pieces are also emitted kind by\n72\t * kind rather than row by row, so each kind shares one overlay layer.\n73\t *\n74\t * Why it is a still. The scout's decisive find was a constraint, not an\n75\t * opportunity: this audience distrusts animated charts (\"They're\n76\t * entertainment, not a tool for seriously comparing data\", HN 30268920), and\n77\t * nobody was asking for an animated benchmark clip. So the still IS the\n78\t * product — the picture you commit next to the README. It also sidesteps a\n79\t * real defect: an animated card whose motion builds from nothing renders a\n80\t * near-blank browse still, because the browse still is frame 0.\n81\t *\n82\t * Day 002 of one-a-day. Scouted from GitHub and HN: the rows already exist\n83\t * (`github-action-benchmark` needs `name`, `unit`, `value` + optional\n84\t * `range`), the picture does not, and the current answer is a hand-committed\n85\t * SVG or `plot_whisker.py` + numpy + matplotlib + scipy.\n86\t */\n87\t\n88\t/** One benchmark case: the two runs being compared, and how much each moved. */\n89\texport type BenchRow = {\n90\t  /** The benchmark's name, as the harness prints it. */\n91\t  name: string;\n92\t  /** Unit shown after the numbers (\"ms\", \"MB\", \"ops/s\"). Per row: units may differ. */\n93\t  unit?: string;\n94\t  /** The baseline measurement. Must be > 0 — every bar on the row is relative to it. */\n95\t  base: number;\n96\t  /** Run-to-run variance of the baseline, as +/- this much. Omit and the row has no verdict. */\n97\t  baseRange?: number;\n98\t  /** The candidate measurement. */\n99\t  value: number;\n100\t  /** Run-to-run variance of the candidate, as +/- this much. */\n101\t  range?: number;\n102\t};\n103\t\n104\texport type BenchDeltaProps = {\n105\t  /** The benchmark cases. Up to 8 are drawn; the footer says so when more are given. */\n106\t  rows: BenchRow[];\n107\t  /** Headline: what is being compared. */\n108\t  title?: string;\n109\t  /** The conditions caveat - machine, run count, cache state. Empty removes the line. */\n110\t  subtitle?: string;\n111\t  /** Name of the baseline run; it labels the rule. */\n112\t  baselineLabel?: string;\n113\t  /** Name of the candidate run. */\n114\t  candidateLabel?: string;\n115\t  /** True when a LOWER number is better (times, bytes). False for throughput. */\n116\t  smallerIsBetter?: boolean;\n117\t  /** Hand-tuned background / ink / muted trio. */\n118\t  preset?: \"dark\" | \"light\";\n119\t  /** The colour an improvement is drawn in (#rrggbb). */\n120\t  accent?: string;\n121\t  /** Dev-only: check the layout contract and draw it over the card. */\n122\t  debugLayout?: boolean;\n123\t};\n124\t\n125\tconst ID = \"@one-a-day/dev/bench-delta/v1\";\n126\tconst HEX = /^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/;\n127\t\n128\t/** Drawn at most this many rows; the rest are counted in the footer. */\n129\texport const MAX_ROWS = 8;\n130\t/**\n131\t * The rule sits where the longest bar on the card still fits the track, but a\n132\t * single catastrophic regression must not shrink every other bar to a stub -\n133\t * so the scale stops widening here and an over-long bar is clamped to the\n134\t * track. The printed ratio is always the true one.\n135\t */\n136\tconst MAX_REL = 3.2;\n137\t/** Headroom past the longest bar so it never touches the track's edge. */\n138\tconst REL_PAD = 1.06;\n139\t\n140\t/** Hand-tuned trios: dark is tuned, not derived from light. */\n141\tconst PRESETS = {\n142\t  dark: {\n143\t    bg: \"#0d1117\" as MosaicColor,\n144\t    ink: \"#e6edf3\" as MosaicColor,\n145\t    dim: \"#8b949e\" as MosaicColor,\n146\t    base: \"#6b7784\" as MosaicColor,\n147\t    worse: \"#f85149\" as MosaicColor,\n148\t    noise: \"#d29922\" as MosaicColor,\n149\t    unknown: \"#4a545f\" as MosaicColor,\n150\t    rule: \"#adbac7\" as MosaicColor,\n151\t  },\n152\t  light: {\n153\t    bg: \"#ffffff\" as MosaicColor,\n154\t    ink: \"#1f2328\" as MosaicColor,\n155\t    dim: \"#59636e\" as MosaicColor,\n156\t    base: \"#9fa9b4\" as MosaicColor,\n157\t    worse: \"#cf222e\" as MosaicColor,\n158\t    noise: \"#9a6700\" as MosaicColor,\n159\t    unknown: \"#c9d0d7\" as MosaicColor,\n160\t    rule: \"#424a53\" as MosaicColor,\n161\t  },\n162\t};\n163\t\n164\tconst DEFAULT_ACCENT = \"#3fb950\";\n165\tconst DEFAULT_TITLE = \"v1.5 vs v1.4\";\n166\tconst DEFAULT_SUBTITLE = \"best of 10 runs - 8-core M4 Pro - cold caches\";\n167\tconst DEFAULT_BASELINE = \"v1.4\";\n168\tconst DEFAULT_CANDIDATE = \"v1.5\";\n169\t\n170\t/**\n171\t * The default card IS the argument, and it is chosen to make every state the\n172\t * template can reach visible with no inputs: a clear win whose intervals are\n173\t * far apart; a 4% \"improvement\" whose intervals overlap, so it is noise; a\n174\t * regression that crosses the rule; a row measured once on each side, so\n175\t * there is no spread at all; and a row where only the BASELINE was measured\n176\t * repeatedly - a 1.42x gap that still earns no claim, because one run is not\n177\t * a measurement.\n178\t */\n179\texport const SAMPLE_ROWS: BenchRow[] = [\n180\t  { name: \"parse 1MB json\", unit: \"ms\", base: 412, baseRange: 14, value: 171, range: 6 },\n181\t  { name: \"render 10k rows\", unit: \"ms\", base: 88.2, baseRange: 9.6, value: 84.6, range: 8.4 },\n182\t  { name: \"cold start\", unit: \"ms\", base: 1240, baseRange: 61, value: 1395, range: 64 },\n183\t  { name: \"gzip 4MB\", unit: \"ms\", base: 59, value: 44.5 },\n184\t  { name: \"resolve imports\", unit: \"ms\", base: 210, baseRange: 9, value: 148 },\n185\t];\n186\t\n187\tconst propsSchema = definePropsSchema<BenchDeltaProps>({\n188\t  rows: {\n189\t    type: \"list\",\n190\t    required: true,\n191\t    description:\n192\t      \"The benchmark cases, in the order they should read. Each row is the pair of runs for one benchmark: name, unit, the baseline number and the candidate number, plus the run-to-run variance of each (+/-). A row with no variance on either side gets no verdict - that is deliberate. Up to 8 rows are drawn.\",\n193\t    meta: { constraints: { maxItems: 32 }, ui: { label: \"Rows\", order: 1, primary: true } },\n194\t    fields: {\n195\t      name: { type: \"string\", required: true, description: \"The benchmark's name, as the harness prints it.\", meta: { ui: { label: \"Name\" } } },\n196\t      unit: { type: \"string\", required: false, description: 'Unit shown after the numbers (\"ms\", \"MB\", \"ops/s\"). Units may differ per row - bars are ratios, not magnitudes.', meta: { control: { placeholder: \"none\" }, ui: { label: \"Unit\" } } },\n197\t      base: { type: \"number\", required: true, description: \"The baseline measurement. Must be greater than zero: the whole row is drawn relative to it.\", meta: { ui: { label: \"Baseline\" } } },\n198\t      baseRange: { type: \"number\", required: false, description: \"Run-to-run variance of the baseline, as +/- this much (the same field github-action-benchmark calls range). BOTH sides need one before the row gets a verdict; 0 is a valid answer and means the metric is deterministic.\", meta: { constraints: { min: 0 }, control: { placeholder: \"none\" }, ui: { label: \"Baseline +/-\" } } },\n199\t      value: { type: \"number\", required: true, description: \"The candidate measurement.\", meta: { ui: { label: \"Candidate\" } } },\n200\t      range: { type: \"number\", required: false, description: \"Run-to-run variance of the candidate, as +/- this much. A string is accepted too - github-action-benchmark declares this field a string and emits \\\"3\\\".\", meta: { constraints: { min: 0 }, control: { placeholder: \"none\" }, ui: { label: \"Candidate +/-\" } } },"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"679f0a15-4f8f-4716-bdc7-19821eac62a2","uuid":"aefc5299-9ffb-4cad-a490-179ea6260c02","timestamp":"2026-10-02T17:24:30.134Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\src\\dev\\bench-delta\\v1\\bench-delta.ts","content":"import type {\n  MosaicColor,\n  MosaicDocument,\n  MosaicEngineContext,\n  MosaicSource,\n} from \"@m0saic/types\";\nimport { asTemplateId } from \"@m0saic/types\";\nimport { toM0String } from \"@m0saic/dsl-stdlib\";\nimport {\n  bindProp,\n  bindPropPath,\n  defineMosaicTemplate,\n  definePropsSchema,\n  makeColorTile,\n  placeInsetPieces,\n  tag,\n} from \"@m0saic/template-utils\";\nimport type { LayoutConstraint } from \"@m0saic/template-utils\";\nimport { textFitsMeasured, withLayoutIntent } from \"../../../_shared/layout\";\nimport { budget, ellipsize, textCell, widthOf, wrapFit } from \"../../../_shared/text\";\nimport { whyTutorial } from \"../../../_shared/why\";\nimport type { WhySpec } from \"../../../_shared/why\";\n\n/**\n * `@one-a-day/dev/bench-delta/v1` — the before/after benchmark card, and\n * whether the change is real.\n *\n * ONE CONCEPT: **the rectangles carry the claim.** Every row's BASELINE bar\n * ends at the same x, so the whole card has ONE vertical rule, and the\n * CANDIDATE bar is drawn relative to its own baseline — so a bar's length is\n * the speedup and nothing else. Ratios, not magnitudes, are the shared\n * channel, which is what lets ns, MB and percent sit honestly on one card;\n * the magnitudes stay as printed text in the label column, where mixed units\n * cost nothing. Under the default smaller-is-better, a bar that crosses the\n * rule is a regression, readable as a shape before any label is read (with\n * `smallerIsBetter: false` the geometry is byte-identical and crossing is the\n * win — the footer states which direction the card is scoring).\n *\n * The second rectangle on each bar is the run-to-run interval — `range`, the\n * same optional field `github-action-benchmark` already carries — and the\n * VERDICT is derived from it, never from the ratio alone: disjoint intervals\n * are `better` / `worse`, overlapping intervals are `within noise`, and a row\n * where EITHER side failed to declare a range gets no verdict at all. That\n * last rule is the opinion this template exists to hold. A candidate run once\n * has not been measured, and silence on one side is not a claim of exactness,\n * so it earns no pill. An explicit `0` IS a claim — a deterministic metric\n * gets its verdict. For contrast, the action's own regression check fires when\n * a number is \"worse than the previous exceeding 200% threshold\", which has no\n * notion of noise at all.\n *\n * What the card computes is weaker than significance, so the footer says so\n * out loud: *a verdict means the two +/- ranges do not overlap*. A card that\n * names its own test can be argued with; one that only prints a verdict\n * cannot.\n *\n * The rule that bites: **svg text never wraps or shrinks by itself, and\n * benchmark names are arbitrary user strings** (\"My Custom Smaller Is Better\n * Benchmark - CPU Load\" is the real example in the action's own README). Every\n * cell is measured against the bundled font, one shared size per COLUMN so\n * neighbouring rows never differ, and the degrade ladder is: shrink, wrap to\n * two lines, WIDEN the label column, drop whole lines, and only then — at the\n * 480x270 contract floor, where the box holds one line of 7px type and the\n * alternative is dropping the benchmark — ellipsize. The footer degrades by\n * dropping whole clauses, lowest value first, so its disclosures survive; the\n * values line drops the unit before it would ever truncate a digit.\n *\n * Nothing here is drawn that carries no data, and that is a performance\n * contract as much as a design one: a decorative full-track wash under every\n * bar kept the tiles off the engine's grid sheet and pushed the overlay chain\n * to 27 at five rows — past the ~25 where ffmpeg SILENTLY degrades inline\n * masks, which is every glyph on this card. Pieces are also emitted kind by\n * kind rather than row by row, so each kind shares one overlay layer.\n *\n * Why it is a still. The scout's decisive find was a constraint, not an\n * opportunity: this audience distrusts animated charts (\"They're\n * entertainment, not a tool for seriously comparing data\", HN 30268920), and\n * nobody was asking for an animated benchmark clip. So the still IS the\n * product — the picture you commit next to the README. It also sidesteps a\n * real defect: an animated card whose motion builds from nothing renders a\n * near-blank browse still, because the browse still is frame 0.\n *\n * Day 002 of one-a-day. Scouted from GitHub and HN: the rows already exist\n * (`github-action-benchmark` needs `name`, `unit`, `value` + optional\n * `range`), the picture does not, and the current answer is a hand-committed\n * SVG or `plot_whisker.py` + numpy + matplotlib + scipy.\n */\n\n/** One benchmark case: the two runs being compared, and how much each moved. */\nexport type BenchRow = {\n  /** The benchmark's name, as the harness prints it. */\n  name: string;\n  /** Unit shown after the numbers (\"ms\", \"MB\", \"ops/s\"). Per row: units may differ. */\n  unit?: string;\n  /** The baseline measurement. Must be > 0 — every bar on the row is relative to it. */\n  base: number;\n  /** Run-to-run variance of the baseline, as +/- this much. Omit and the row has no verdict. */\n  baseRange?: number;\n  /** The candidate measurement. */\n  value: number;\n  /** Run-to-run variance of the candidate, as +/- this much. */\n  range?: number;\n};\n\nexport type BenchDeltaProps = {\n  /** The benchmark cases. Up to 8 are drawn; the footer says so when more are given. */\n  rows: BenchRow[];\n  /** Headline: what is being compared. */\n  title?: string;\n  /** The conditions caveat - machine, run count, cache state. Empty removes the line. */\n  subtitle?: string;\n  /** Name of the baseline run; it labels the rule. */\n  baselineLabel?: string;\n  /** Name of the candidate run. */\n  candidateLabel?: string;\n  /** True when a LOWER number is better (times, bytes). False for throughput. */\n  smallerIsBetter?: boolean;\n  /** Hand-tuned background / ink / muted trio. */\n  preset?: \"dark\" | \"light\";\n  /** The colour an improvement is drawn in (#rrggbb). */\n  accent?: string;\n  /** Dev-only: check the layout contract and draw it over the card. */\n  debugLayout?: boolean;\n};\n\nconst ID = \"@one-a-day/dev/bench-delta/v1\";\nconst HEX = /^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/;\n\n/** Drawn at most this many rows; the rest are counted in the footer. */\nexport const MAX_ROWS = 8;\n/**\n * The rule sits where the longest bar on the card still fits the track, but a\n * single catastrophic regression must not shrink every other bar to a stub -\n * so the scale stops widening here and an over-long bar is clamped to the\n * track. The printed ratio is always the true one.\n */\nconst MAX_REL = 3.2;\n/** Headroom past the longest bar so it never touches the track's edge. */\nconst REL_PAD = 1.06;\n\n/** Hand-tuned trios: dark is tuned, not derived from light. */\nconst PRESETS = {\n  dark: {\n    bg: \"#0d1117\" as MosaicColor,\n    ink: \"#e6edf3\" as MosaicColor,\n    dim: \"#8b949e\" as MosaicColor,\n    base: \"#6b7784\" as MosaicColor,\n    worse: \"#f85149\" as MosaicColor,\n    noise: \"#d29922\" as MosaicColor,\n    unknown: \"#4a545f\" as MosaicColor,\n    rule: \"#adbac7\" as MosaicColor,\n  },\n  light: {\n    bg: \"#ffffff\" as MosaicColor,\n    ink: \"#1f2328\" as MosaicColor,\n    dim: \"#59636e\" as MosaicColor,\n    base: \"#9fa9b4\" as MosaicColor,\n    worse: \"#cf222e\" as MosaicColor,\n    noise: \"#9a6700\" as MosaicColor,\n    unknown: \"#c9d0d7\" as MosaicColor,\n    rule: \"#424a53\" as MosaicColor,\n  },\n};\n\nconst DEFAULT_ACCENT = \"#3fb950\";\nconst DEFAULT_TITLE = \"v1.5 vs v1.4\";\nconst DEFAULT_SUBTITLE = \"best of 10 runs - 8-core M4 Pro - cold caches\";\nconst DEFAULT_BASELINE = \"v1.4\";\nconst DEFAULT_CANDIDATE = \"v1.5\";\n\n/**\n * The default card IS the argument, and it is chosen to make every state the\n * template can reach visible with no inputs: a clear win whose intervals are\n * far apart; a 4% \"improvement\" whose intervals overlap, so it is noise; a\n * regression that crosses the rule; a row measured once on each side, so\n * there is no spread at all; and a row where only the BASELINE was measured\n * repeatedly - a 1.42x gap that still earns no claim, because one run is not\n * a measurement.\n */\nexport const SAMPLE_ROWS: BenchRow[] = [\n  { name: \"parse 1MB json\", unit: \"ms\", base: 412, baseRange: 14, value: 171, range: 6 },\n  { name: \"render 10k rows\", unit: \"ms\", base: 88.2, baseRange: 9.6, value: 84.6, range: 8.4 },\n  { name: \"cold start\", unit: \"ms\", base: 1240, baseRange: 61, value: 1395, range: 64 },\n  { name: \"gzip 4MB\", unit: \"ms\", base: 59, value: 44.5 },\n  { name: \"resolve imports\", unit: \"ms\", base: 210, baseRange: 9, value: 148 },\n];\n\nconst propsSchema = definePropsSchema<BenchDeltaProps>({\n  rows: {\n    type: \"list\",\n    required: true,\n    description:\n      \"The benchmark cases, in the order they should read. Each row is the pair of runs for one benchmark: name, unit, the baseline number and the candidate number, plus the run-to-run variance of each (+/-). A row with no variance on either side gets no verdict - that is deliberate. Up to 8 rows are drawn.\",\n    meta: { constraints: { maxItems: 32 }, ui: { label: \"Rows\", order: 1, primary: true } },\n    fields: {\n      name: { type: \"string\", required: true, description: \"The benchmark's name, as the harness prints it.\", meta: { ui: { label: \"Name\" } } },\n      unit: { type: \"string\", required: false, description: 'Unit shown after the numbers (\"ms\", \"MB\", \"ops/s\"). Units may differ per row - bars are ratios, not magnitudes.', meta: { control: { placeholder: \"none\" }, ui: { label: \"Unit\" } } },\n      base: { type: \"number\", required: true, description: \"The baseline measurement. Must be greater than zero: the whole row is drawn relative to it.\", meta: { ui: { label: \"Baseline\" } } },\n      baseRange: { type: \"number\", required: false, description: \"Run-to-run variance of the baseline, as +/- this much (the same field github-action-benchmark calls range). BOTH sides need one before the row gets a verdict; 0 is a valid answer and means the metric is deterministic.\", meta: { constraints: { min: 0 }, control: { placeholder: \"none\" }, ui: { label: \"Baseline +/-\" } } },\n      value: { type: \"number\", required: true, description: \"The candidate measurement.\", meta: { ui: { label: \"Candidate\" } } },\n      range: { type: \"number\", required: false, description: \"Run-to-run variance of the candidate, as +/- this much. A string is accepted too - github-action-benchmark declares this field a string and emits \\\"3\\\".\", meta: { constraints: { min: 0 }, control: { placeholder: \"none\" }, ui: { label: \"Candidate +/-\" } } },","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1268}}}
